I would not merge yet. I found one important race family that the A–T tests do not currently cover.

Remote chain movement can still become a terminal indexing error

The intention is that ordinary Core reorganization races become ErrRemoteChainMoved and trigger immediate reconciliation. But there are several windows where they currently do not.

Consider this entirely normal sequence:

Explorer local:
A0 -> A1

reconcile():
Core is still:
A0 -> A1
PASS

Core reorganizes immediately afterward to:
A0 -> B1 -> B2

Indexer now fetches height 2:
GetBlockHash(2) = B2
GetBlock(B2)     = B2, PreviousHash=B1
second GetBlockHash(2) = B2

All RPC race checks pass.

Then Store.ApplyBlock(B2) sees local tip A1. Store correctly requires an immediate child whose PreviousHash == A1, so it returns ErrNonSequentialBlock. Store is behaving exactly as designed here.

But applyHeight() simply wraps that error and returns it, so SyncToTip treats the perfectly normal Core reorg as a fatal integrity failure instead of restarting reconciliation.

There are two related forms of the same problem.

If Core's tip shrinks after GetBlockCount(), a requested GetBlockHash(height) can legitimately become unavailable. Right now that RPC error is also terminal rather than recognized as chain movement.

And after a successful reconcile(), syncToTipLocked() performs a new GetBlockCount() and does:

if local.Height >= target {
    return nil
}

If Core retreats or performs a same-height reorg between those two operations, the explorer can declare itself caught up with a tip that is no longer canonical and then wait the full polling interval. That's contrary to the intended “chain moved → reconcile immediately” behavior.

The current F test covers hash changing between the two GetBlockHash calls inside applyHeight, while G changes Core's branch between two separate SyncToTip calls. Neither exercises the critical reorg-after-reconcile/before-next-child window.

There is also one small operational mistake: runIndex constructs the configured logger, but passes nil into indexer.New, so the indexer falls back to slog.Default() instead of honoring QOGE_LOG_LEVEL / QOGE_LOG_JSON.

