This contains two races.

A. TIP RETREAT

reconcile sees:

  local=100
  Core=100

Core retreats to 90.

Then GetBlockCount returns 90.

Current:

  local.Height >= target

returns nil with local checkpoint still 100.

Instead:

  if local.Height > target:
      immediately restart reconciliation

Do NOT wait for the next polling interval.


B. SAME-HEIGHT REORG

reconcile verifies local hash A100.

Core changes immediately afterward to B100.

GetBlockCount still returns 100.

Current code returns nil without rechecking the hash.

When:

  local.Height == target

perform a final canonical-hash confirmation:

  currentCoreHash = GetBlockHash(local.Height)

Require:

  currentCoreHash == local.Hash

before declaring caught up.

If it differs:
  restart reconciliation immediately.

If GetBlockHash becomes unavailable because Core retreated:
  classify that as ErrRemoteChainMoved as described above.

It is impossible to make Core/PostgreSQL atomically share a snapshot;
the purpose is to close the obvious orchestration windows and make the
